.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_PKEY_VERIFY 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_PKEY_verify_init ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex2 ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_verify ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_verify_message_update ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_verify_message_final ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature
.Nd signature verification using a public key algorithm
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/evp.h
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_verify_init
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex2
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "EVP_SIGNATURE *algo"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "EVP_SIGNATURE *algo"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx"
.Fa "const unsigned char *sig"
.Fa "size_t siglen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_verify_message_update
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned char *in"
.Fa "size_t inlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_verify_message_final
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_verify
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "const unsigned char *sig"
.Fa "size_t siglen"
.Fa "const unsigned char *tbs"
.Fa "size_t tbslen"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init
initializes a public key algorithm context
.Fa ctx
for verification using the algorithm given when the context was created
using
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_new 3
or variants thereof.
The algorithm is used to fetch a
.Vt EVP_SIGNATURE
method implicitly, see
.Xr provider 7
for more information about implicit fetches.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex
is the same as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init
but additionally sets the passed parameters
.Em params
on the context before returning.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex2
is the same as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex ,
but works with an explicitly fetched
.Vt EVP_SIGNATURE
.Fa algo .
A context
.Fa ctx
without a pre-loaded key cannot be used with this function.
Depending on what algorithm was fetched, certain details revolving
around the treatment of the input to
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify
may be pre-determined, and in that case, those details may normally not
be changed.
See
.Sx NOTES
below for a deeper explanation.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init
initializes a public key algorithm context
.Fa ctx
for verifying an unlimited size message using the algorithm given by
.Fa algo
and the key given through
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_new 3
or
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_pkey 3 .
Passing the message is supported both in a one-shot fashion using
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify ,
and through the combination of
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_update 3
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_final 3 .
This function enables using algorithms that can process input of
arbitrary length, such as ED25519, RSA-SHA256 and similar.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature
specifies the
.Fa siglen
bytes long signature
.Fa sig
to be verified against by
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_final 3 .
It
.Em must
be used together with
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_update 3
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_final 3 .
See
.Sx NOTES
below for a deeper explanation.
.Pp
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_update 3
adds
.Fa inlen
bytes from
.Fa in
to the data to be processed for verification.
The signature algorithm specification and implementation determine how
the input bytes are processed and if there's a limit on the total size
of the input.
See
.Sx NOTES
below for a deeper explanation.
.Pp
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_final 3
verifies the processed data, given only
.Fa ctx .
The signature to verify against must have been given with
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify
is a one-shot function that performs the same thing as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature
call with
.Fa sig
and
.Fa siglen
as parameters, followed by a single
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_update 3
call with
.Fa tbs
and
.Fa tbslen ,
followed by
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_final 3
call.
.Sh NOTES
.Ss General
Some signature implementations only accumulate the input data and do no
further processing before verifying it (they expect the input to be a
digest), while others compress the data, typically by internally
producing a digest, and signing the result, which is then verified
against a given signature.
Some of them support both modes of operation at the same time.
The caller is expected to know how the chosen algorithm is supposed to
behave and under what conditions.
.Pp
For example, an RSA implementation can be expected to only expect a
digest as input, while ED25519 can be expected to process the input with
a hash, i.e.
to produce the digest internally, and while RSA-SHA256 can be expected
to handle either mode of operation, depending on if the operation was
initialized with
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex2
or with
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init .
.Pp
Similarly, an RSA implementation usually expects additional details to
be set, like the message digest algorithm that the input is supposed to
be digested with, as well as the padding mode (see
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature_md 3
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_rsa_padding 3
and similar others), while an RSA-SHA256 implementation usually has
these details pre-set and immutable.
.Pp
The functions described here can't be used to combine separate
algorithms.
In particular, neither
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature_md 3
nor the
.Sy OSSL_PARAM
parameter "digest"
.Pf ( Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_DIGEST )
can be used to combine a signature algorithm with a hash algorithm to
process the input.
In other words, it's not possible to specify a
.Fa ctx
pre-loaded with an RSA pkey, or an
.Fa algo
that fetched
.Qo Li RSA Qc
and try to specify SHA256 separately to get the functionality of
RSA-SHA256. If combining algorithms in that manner is desired, please
use
.Xr EVP_DigestVerifyInit 3
and associated functions, or
.Xr EVP_VerifyInit 3
and associated functions.
.Ss Performing multiple verifications
When initialized using
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex
or
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex2 ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify
can be called more than once on the same context to have several
one-shot operations performed using the same parameters.
.Pp
When initialized using
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init ,
it's not possible to call
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify
multiple times.
.Ss \&On EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature()
Some signature algorithms (such as LMS) require the signature
verification data be specified before verifying the message.
Other algorithms allow the signature to be specified late.
To allow either way (which may depend on the application's flow of
input), the signature to be verified against
.Em must
be specified using this function when using
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_message_update
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_message_final
to perform the verification.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
All functions return 1 for success and 0 or a negative value for
failure.
However, unlike other functions, the return value 0 from
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_recover 3
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_verify_message_final
only indicates that the signature did not verify successfully (that is
tbs did not match the original data or the signature was of invalid
form) it is not an indication of a more serious error.
.Pp
A negative value indicates an error other that signature verification
failure.
In particular a return value of -2 indicates the operation is not
supported by the public key algorithm.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss RSA with PKCS#1 padding for SHA256
Verify signature using PKCS#1 padding and a SHA256 digest as input:
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>
 #include <openssl/rsa.h>

 EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx;
 unsigned char *md, *sig;
 size_t mdlen, siglen;
 EVP_PKEY *verify_key;

 /*
  * NB: assumes verify_key, sig, siglen md and mdlen are already set up
  * and that verify_key is an RSA public key
  */
 ctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new(verify_key, NULL /* no engine */);
 if (ctx == NULL)
     /* Error occurred */
 if (EVP_PKEY_verify_init(ctx) <= 0)
     /* Error */
 if (EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_rsa_padding(ctx, RSA_PKCS1_PADDING) <= 0)
     /* Error */
 if (EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature_md(ctx, EVP_sha256()) <= 0)
     /* Error */

 /* Perform operation */
 ret = EVP_PKEY_verify(ctx, sig, siglen, md, mdlen);

 /*
  * ret == 1 indicates success, 0 verify failure and < 0 for some
  * other error.
  */
.Ed
.Ss RSA-SHA256 with a pre-computed digest
Verify a digest with RSA-SHA256 using one-shot functions.
To be noted is that RSA-SHA256 is assumed to be an implementation of
.Qo Li sha256WithRSAEncryption Qc ,
for which the padding is pre-determined to be
.Sy RSA_PKCS1_PADDING ,
and the input digest is assumed to have been computed using SHA256.
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>
 #include <openssl/rsa.h>

 EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx;
 /* md is a SHA-256 digest in this example. */
 unsigned char *md, *sig;
 size_t mdlen = 32, siglen;
 EVP_PKEY *signing_key;

 /*
  * NB: assumes verify_key, sig, siglen, md and mdlen are already set up
  * and that verify_key is an RSA public key
  */
 ctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new(signing_key, NULL /* no engine */);
 alg = EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch(NULL, "RSA-SHA256", NULL);

 if (ctx == NULL)
     /* Error occurred */
 if (EVP_PKEY_verify_init_ex2(ctx, alg, NULL) <= 0)
     /* Error */

 /* Determine buffer length */
 if (EVP_PKEY_verify(ctx, sig, siglen, md, mdlen) <= 0)
     /* Error or signature doesn't verify */

 /* Perform operation */
 ret = EVP_PKEY_verify(ctx, sig, siglen, md, mdlen);

 /*
  * ret == 1 indicates success, 0 verify failure and < 0 for some
  * other error.
  */
.Ed
.Ss RSA-SHA256, one-shot
Verify a document with RSA-SHA256 using one-shot functions.
To be noted is that RSA-SHA256 is assumed to be an implementation of
.Qo Li sha256WithRSAEncryption Qc ,
for which the padding is pre-determined to be
.Sy RSA_PKCS1_PADDING .
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>
 #include <openssl/rsa.h>

 EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx;
 /* in the input in this example. */
 unsigned char *in, *sig;
 /* inlen is the length of the input in this example. */
 size_t inlen, siglen;
 EVP_PKEY *signing_key;
 EVP_SIGNATURE *alg;

 /*
  * NB: assumes signing_key, in and inlen are set up before
  * the next step. signing_key must be an RSA private key,
  * in must point to data to be digested and signed, and
  * inlen must be the size of the data in bytes.
  */
 ctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new(signing_key, NULL /* no engine */);
 alg = EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch(NULL, "RSA-SHA256", NULL);

 if (ctx == NULL || alg == NULL)
     /* Error occurred */
 if (EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init(ctx, alg, NULL) <= 0)
     /* Error */

 /* Perform operation */
 ret = EVP_PKEY_verify(ctx, sig, siglen, in, inlen);

 /*
  * ret == 1 indicates success, 0 verify failure and < 0 for some
  * other error.
  */
.Ed
.Ss RSA-SHA256, using update and final
This is the same as the previous example, but allowing stream-like
functionality.
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>
 #include <openssl/rsa.h>

 EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx;
 /* in is the input in this example. */
 unsigned char *in, *sig;
 /* inlen is the length of the input in this example. */
 size_t inlen, siglen;
 EVP_PKEY *signing_key;
 EVP_SIGNATURE *alg;

 /*
  * NB: assumes signing_key, in and inlen are set up before
  * the next step. signing_key must be an RSA private key,
  * in must point to data to be digested and signed, and
  * inlen must be the size of the data in bytes.
  */
 ctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new(signing_key, NULL /* no engine */);
 alg = EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch(NULL, "RSA-SHA256", NULL);

 if (ctx == NULL || alg == NULL)
     /* Error occurred */
 if (EVP_PKEY_verify_message_init(ctx, alg, NULL) <= 0)
     /* Error */

 /* We have the signature, specify it early */
 EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_signature(ctx, sig, siglen);

 /* Perform operation */
 while (inlen > 0) {
     if (EVP_PKEY_verify_message_update(ctx, in, inlen)) <= 0)
         /* Error */
     if (inlen > 256) {
         inlen -= 256;
         in += 256;
     } else {
         inlen = 0;
     }
 }
 ret = EVP_PKEY_verify_message_final(ctx);

 /*
  * ret == 1 indicates success, 0 verify failure and < 0 for some
  * other error.
  */
.Ed
